<section class="ulam-page">
  <h1>The Ulam Spiral</h1>

  <p>
    In 1963, mathematician
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanis%C5%82aw_Ulam" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stanisław Ulam</a>
    was sitting through a dull talk and started doodling: write the natural numbers in a
    square spiral, then circle the primes. Unexpectedly, the primes lined up along
    diagonals and other striking patterns — a picture now called the
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulam_spiral" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ulam spiral</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Below, every cell is an integer from 1 (centre) outward. Primes glow; composites
    stay quiet. Crank the side length up and the diagonals keep reappearing — a reminder
    that “random-looking” primes still have geometric structure when you lay them out
    this way.
  </p>

  <div class="ulam-controls" role="group" aria-label="Spiral options">
    <label class="ulam-control">
      <span>Side length <strong id="ulam-side-value">101</strong> (max <span id="ulam-max-value">10201</span>)</span>
      <input id="ulam-side" type="range" min="5" max="301" step="2" value="101" />
    </label>

    <label class="ulam-control">
      <span>Colour</span>
      <select id="ulam-colour">
        <option value="primes" selected>Primes only</option>
        <option value="rainbow">Rainbow by value</option>
        <option value="factors">By divisor count</option>
      </select>
    </label>

    <label class="ulam-control ulam-checkbox">
      <input id="ulam-composites" type="checkbox" checked />
      <span>Show composites</span>
    </label>
  </div>

  <p id="ulam-stats" class="ulam-stats">Building spiral…</p>
  <p id="ulam-hover" class="ulam-hover" aria-live="polite">Hover a cell to see its number.</p>

  <div id="ulam-chart" class="ulam-chart"></div>

  <h2>What you’re seeing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Start at 1 in the centre, step right to 2, up to 3, left, left, down, down, right, right… doubling the run length every two turns.</li>
    <li>Many primes fall on lines like <em>4n² − 2n + 1</em> — Euler’s polynomial <em>n² − n + 41</em> is a famous related curiosity.</li>
    <li>The spiral doesn’t prove a deep theorem by itself; it’s a striking way to <em>see</em> structure in the primes.</li>
  </ul>

  {{> showSource }}
</section>

{{> require}}

